<section class="section reveal" id="eigenvalue-decomposition">
  <span class="section-label">Fundamentals</span>
  <h2>Eigenvalue Decomposition and Diagonalization</h2>

  <p>Eigenvalue decomposition reveals the structure of a matrix. For symmetric matrices (common in AI), it provides an orthogonal basis where the matrix becomes diagonal. This is the foundation for PCA, spectral clustering, and understanding dynamical systems.</p>

  <div class="analogy">
    <h4>💡 Think of It This Way</h4>
    <p>Imagine a transformation that stretches space. Eigenvalues tell you how much stretching happens along each principal direction (eigenvector). A diagonal matrix is the simplest form—stretching happens only along the axes, no rotation. Eigenvalue decomposition finds this simplest representation.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Computing and Using Eigenvalue Decomposition</h3>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Eigenvalue decomposition</span>
<span class="kw">import</span> <span class="im">numpy</span> <span class="kw">as</span> <span class="im">np</span>

<span class="cm"># Create a symmetric matrix (guaranteed real eigenvalues)</span>
<span class="va">A</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([[<span class="nm">4</span>, <span class="nm">1</span>], [<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">3</span>]])

<span class="cm"># Eigenvalue decomposition: A = Q * Lambda * Q^T</span>
<span class="va">eigenvalues</span>, <span class="va">Q</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">linalg</span>.<span class="fn">eigh</span>(<span class="va">A</span>)  <span class="cm"># eigh for symmetric matrices</span>

<span class="cm"># Q contains eigenvectors as columns</span>
<span class="cm"># eigenvalues are the diagonal elements</span>

<span class="cm"># Reconstruct original matrix</span>
<span class="va">Lambda</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">diag</span>(<span class="va">eigenvalues</span>)
<span class="va">A_reconstructed</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">Q</span> <span class="op">@</span> <span class="va">Lambda</span> <span class="op">@</span> <span class="va">Q</span>.<span class="fn">T</span>

<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">"Original A:"</span>)
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">A</span>)
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">"Reconstructed A:"</span>)
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="va">A_reconstructed</span>)
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">"Match:"</span>, <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">allclose</span>(<span class="va">A</span>, <span class="va">A_reconstructed</span>))</code></pre>

  <h3>Application: Power Iteration for Dominant Eigenvalue</h3>

  <p>The dominant eigenvalue (largest absolute value) is crucial for understanding matrix behavior. Power iteration is a simple method to compute it without full decomposition.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Power iteration to find dominant eigenvalue</span>
<span class="va">A</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([[<span class="nm">4</span>, <span class="nm">1</span>], [<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">3</span>]])

<span class="cm"># Start with random vector</span>
<span class="va">v</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">random</span>.<span class="fn">randn</span>(<span class="nm">2</span>)
<span class="va">v</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">v</span> <span class="op">/</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">linalg</span>.<span class="fn">norm</span>(<span class="va">v</span>)

<span class="cm"># Iterate: v <- A @ v / ||A @ v||</span>
<span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">_</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">range</span>(<span class="nm">10</span>):
    <span class="va">v</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">A</span> <span class="op">@</span> <span class="va">v</span>
    <span class="va">v</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">v</span> <span class="op">/</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">linalg</span>.<span class="fn">norm</span>(<span class="va">v</span>)

<span class="cm"># Dominant eigenvalue</span>
<span class="va">dominant_eigenvalue</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">v</span> <span class="op">@</span> (<span class="va">A</span> <span class="op">@</span> <span class="va">v</span>)

<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Dominant eigenvalue: {dominant_eigenvalue:.4f}"</span>)
<span class="cm"># Compare with full eigenvalue decomposition</span>
<span class="va">true_dominant</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">max</span>(<span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">linalg</span>.<span class="fn">eigvals</span>(<span class="va">A</span>))
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"True dominant eigenvalue: {true_dominant:.4f}"</span>)</code></pre>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Eigenvalue decomposition diagonalizes symmetric matrices</li>
      <li>Eigenvalues reveal scaling; eigenvectors reveal directions</li>
      <li>Use eigh for symmetric matrices (faster, guaranteed real eigenvalues)</li>
      <li>Dominant eigenvalue determines matrix growth rate</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

