<section class="section reveal" id="svd">
  <span class="section-label">Core Concept</span>
  <h2>Singular Value Decomposition (SVD)</h2>

  <p>SVD is the most important matrix decomposition in machine learning. Every matrix (square or rectangular) has an SVD. It reveals the fundamental structure: directions of maximum variation and their magnitudes.</p>

  <h3>SVD Formula and Interpretation</h3>

  <p>Any matrix A can be written as: A = U * Σ * V^T, where U and V are orthogonal, and Σ is diagonal.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Singular Value Decomposition</span>
<span class="va">A</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([[<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="nm">0</span>],
                [<span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>, <span class="nm">0</span>]])  <span class="cm"># Shape: (2, 3)</span>

<span class="cm"># SVD: A = U @ diag(s) @ V.T</span>
<span class="va">U</span>, <span class="va">s</span>, <span class="va">Vt</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">linalg</span>.<span class="fn">svd</span>(<span class="va">A</span>)

<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"U shape: {U.shape}"</span>)          <span class="cm"># (2, 2)</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Singular values: {s}"</span>)       <span class="cm"># [2., 1.]</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"V^T shape: {Vt.shape}"</span>)       <span class="cm"># (3, 3) or (2, 3) with full=False</span>

<span class="cm"># Reconstruct (with full=False)</span>
<span class="va">U_reduced</span>, <span class="va">s</span>, <span class="va">Vt_reduced</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">linalg</span>.<span class="fn">svd</span>(<span class="va">A</span>, <span class="va">full_matrices</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="kw">False</span>)
<span class="va">A_reconstructed</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">U_reduced</span> <span class="op">@</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">diag</span>(<span class="va">s</span>) <span class="op">@</span> <span class="va">Vt_reduced</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">"Match:"</span>, <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">allclose</span>(<span class="va">A</span>, <span class="va">A_reconstructed</span>))</code></pre>

  <h3>Application: Principal Component Analysis (PCA)</h3>

  <p>PCA reduces dimensionality while preserving variance. SVD is the efficient way to implement it.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># PCA using SVD</span>
<span class="cm"># Data: (n_samples, n_features)</span>
<span class="va">X</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">random</span>.<span class="fn">randn</span>(<span class="nm">100</span>, <span class="nm">10</span>)

<span class="cm"># Center the data (mean = 0)</span>
<span class="va">X_centered</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">X</span> <span class="op">-</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">mean</span>(<span class="va">X</span>, <span class="va">axis</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0</span>)

<span class="cm"># SVD of centered data</span>
<span class="va">U</span>, <span class="va">s</span>, <span class="va">Vt</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">linalg</span>.<span class="fn">svd</span>(<span class="va">X_centered</span>, <span class="va">full_matrices</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="kw">False</span>)

<span class="cm"># Singular values relate to variance</span>
<span class="va">variance</span> <span class="op">=</span> (<span class="va">s</span> <span class="op">**</span> <span class="nm">2</span>) <span class="op">/</span> (<span class="fn">len</span>(<span class="va">X</span>) <span class="op">-</span> <span class="nm">1</span>)
<span class="va">variance_explained</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">variance</span> <span class="op">/</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">sum</span>(<span class="va">variance</span>)

<span class="cm"># Keep first k components explaining 95% of variance</span>
<span class="va">k</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">argmax</span>(<span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">cumsum</span>(<span class="va">variance_explained</span>) <span class="op">></span> <span class="nm">0.95</span>) <span class="op">+</span> <span class="nm">1</span>
<span class="va">components</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">Vt</span>[:<span class="va">k</span>, :]  <span class="cm"># Principal components (k, n_features)</span>

<span class="cm"># Project to PCA space (reduce dimensions)</span>
<span class="va">X_pca</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">X_centered</span> <span class="op">@</span> <span class="va">components</span>.<span class="fn">T</span>  <span class="cm"># Shape: (100, k)</span>

<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Original shape: {X.shape}"</span>)
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Reduced shape: {X_pca.shape}"</span>)
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"Variance explained: {np.sum(variance_explained[:k]):.2%}"</span>)</code></pre>

  <div class="why-matters">
    <h4>🌍 Why This Matters</h4>
    <p><strong>In Machine Learning:</strong> SVD is the foundation for dimensionality reduction, data compression, and feature extraction. Many algorithms (PCA, recommender systems, topic modeling) rely on SVD.</p>
    <p><strong>In Numerical Computing:</strong> Singular values reveal matrix rank and condition number—critical for understanding numerical stability of algorithms.</p>
    <p><strong>In Signal Processing:</strong> SVD separates signal from noise by thresholding small singular values.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>SVD works on any matrix (square or rectangular)</li>
      <li>Singular values are always non-negative and ordered descending</li>
      <li>First k singular values/vectors capture most data variance</li>
      <li>Use SVD for PCA, compression, and rank estimation</li>
      <li>Use full_matrices=False for efficiency with tall/wide matrices</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

