<section class="section reveal" id="qr-factorization">
  <span class="section-label">Advanced</span>
  <h2>QR Factorization and Orthogonal Matrices</h2>

  <p>QR factorization decomposes a matrix into an orthogonal matrix (Q) and an upper triangular matrix (R). It's efficient for solving least-squares problems and orthogonalizing vectors.</p>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># QR factorization</span>
<span class="va">A</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([[<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>], [<span class="nm">3</span>, <span class="nm">4</span>], [<span class="nm">5</span>, <span class="nm">6</span>]])  <span class="cm"># Shape: (3, 2)</span>

<span class="cm"># QR: A = Q @ R</span>
<span class="cm"># Q: orthogonal (m, m) or (m, n) with mode='reduced'</span>
<span class="cm"># R: upper triangular (m, n) or (n, n) with mode='reduced'</span>
<span class="va">Q</span>, <span class="va">R</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">linalg</span>.<span class="fn">qr</span>(<span class="va">A</span>, <span class="va">mode</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'reduced'</span>)

<span class="cm"># Q is orthogonal: Q.T @ Q = I</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">"Q.T @ Q:"</span>)
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">allclose</span>(<span class="va">Q</span>.<span class="fn">T</span> <span class="op">@</span> <span class="va">Q</span>, <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">eye</span>(<span class="nm">2</span>)))  <span class="cm"># True</span>

<span class="cm"># Reconstruct A</span>
<span class="va">A_reconstructed</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">Q</span> <span class="op">@</span> <span class="va">R</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">"Reconstruction match:"</span>, <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">allclose</span>(<span class="va">A</span>, <span class="va">A_reconstructed</span>))</code></pre>

  <h3>Application: Solving Least Squares with QR</h3>

  <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
  <pre><code><span class="cm"># Solving Ax = b using QR factorization</span>
<span class="va">A</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([[<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">0</span>], [<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">1</span>], [<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>]])
<span class="va">b</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">array</span>([<span class="nm">6</span>, <span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="nm">0</span>])

<span class="va">Q</span>, <span class="va">R</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">linalg</span>.<span class="fn">qr</span>(<span class="va">A</span>, <span class="va">mode</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'reduced'</span>)

<span class="cm"># Solve Rx = Q^T b (more stable than using A directly)</span>
<span class="va">x</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">np</span>.<span class="fn">linalg</span>.<span class="fn">solve</span>(<span class="va">R</span>, <span class="va">Q</span>.<span class="fn">T</span> <span class="op">@</span> <span class="va">b</span>)</code></pre>

  <div class="key-takeaway">
    <h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>QR factorization produces orthogonal matrix Q and triangular matrix R</li>
      <li>More numerically stable than solving A directly</li>
      <li>Useful for orthogonalizing column vectors</li>
      <li>Q.T @ Q = I (orthonormal columns)</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

